Historical development:

During the 1980’s: 1G networks were introduced, allowing the first wireless cellular calls using analogue signals. By 1983 Motorola DynaTAC 8000X, the world's first commercially available handheld mobile phone which was designed by a team led by martin cooper, an American engineer at Motorola was produced. It weighed approximately 2.5 pounds and offered around 30 minutes of talk time after a 10hr charge.  

During the 1990s: 2G networks were introduced, making a major shift to digital communication. This enabled text messaging and basic data services while improving call quality and security. By 1998 Nokia 5110 is known for its durable design and long battery life and ease of use. It became a popular mobile phone. Jorma Ollila, Nokia’s CEO from 1992 to 2006, has played an important role in the company’s growth and success in the mobile phone industry.  

During the 2000s: 3G technology began developing, later making faster mobile internet, video calls and improved web browsing more widely available. At this same period of time, Nokia 3310 was also introduced. A highly popular mobile phone recognised for its exceptional durability and impressive battery life. It became famous for its strong build quality and classic games, such as Snake.  

During 2003: Nokia 1100 introduced, designed for simplicity and reliability; the Nokia 1100 featured long battery life, dust resistance and basic functions, including a built-in flashlight. Its affordable price and practical design made it popular among users worldwide. 

During 2004: Motorola RAZR V3, recognised for its thin ultra-thin design and stylish appearance, it became a fashion icon in the mobile phone industry. It showed how mobile phones could combine practical features with modern and attractive designs. 

During 2007: Apple’s first iPhone revolutionised the smartphone industry by introducing a multi-touch screen, removing the traditional physical keyboard, and providing a user-friendly interface. Steve Jobs, Apple’s co-founder and then CEO, introduced the device during a presentation that marked a major milestone in mobile technology. The iPhone helped transform mobile phones into devices for communication, internet browsing and entertainment. 

In the Early 2010s: Smartphones, became mainstream, supported by the expansion of 4G networks. These networks provide faster data speeds and more reliable connections, enabling high-definition video streaming and mobile gaming.

In 2017: iPhone X was introduced with face ID, allowing users to unlock their phones using facing recognition. It also featured an edge-to-edge OLED display and removed the traditional home button, creating a modern smartphone design. It's also known as the 11th generation of iPhone.

By the 2020s till at the present: 5G technology has become increasingly available, offering faster data speeds and more reliable connections. These improvements support advanced applications, including remote patient monitoring, real-time emergency response systems, smart devices, and other connected technologies.  
